    [Performance] Avoid full-buffer copies in compiled embedding token exchange (#19409)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    
    Embedding token exchange mutates slices of persistent, capacity-sized
    buffers. AOT functionalization can rebuild/copy the entire backing
    buffers even for a small decode batch, adding unnecessary memory traffic
    and graph memory.
    
    Use invocation-local padding and masking with the existing group
    AllGather/ReduceScatter APIs. Captured allocations are managed by the
    ACL graph pool. Keep common exchange lengths, output cropping and
    PCP-before-TP reduction; no runner changes or new Embedding custom op.
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    
    Lower compiled embedding overhead without configuration or API changes.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    
    #### End-to-end performance
    
    **DSV4 Flash W4A8, three-round end-to-end comparison:** A3, 8 NPUs,
    MRV2, DP1 × TP1 × PCP8 + EP8, FULL_DECODE_ONLY. Parent main `576b1b0c3`
    vs PR `8ff49a6cd`, with identical hardware/configuration and vLLM
    `a98247ab4` + error-message-only Python 3.11 compatibility commit
    `20804fc0f`.
    
    Each version used two warm-up batches, then three rounds of 8 concurrent
    streaming requests with 32 input / 1,024 output tokens (`temperature=0`,
    `seed=1024`, `ignore_eos=true`). Three-round means:
    
    | Metric | main | PR | Change |
    |---|---:|---:|---:|
    | End-to-end output throughput | 197.40 token/s | 212.01 token/s |
    +7.40% |
    | Request latency | 41.44 s | 38.58 s | -6.90% |
    | TTFT | 443.47 ms | 416.06 ms | -6.18% |
    | Client TPOT | 40.08 ms | 37.31 ms | -6.91% |
    
    End-to-end throughput includes prefill, queueing and stream completion.
    All 48 measured requests passed token-length checks, with zero
    prefix-cache hits/preemptions and no external NPU processes on the
    selected devices. Actual FULL graph replay and the compiled embedding
    exchange path were verified.
    
    Historical DSV4.1 W4A8 measurements against main (A3, PCP8, batch 4,
    65,536 input / 1,024 output tokens, FULL decode):
    
    | Metric | main | Optimized | Reduction |
    |---|---:|---:|---:|
    | Server decode TPOT | 47.492 ms | 40.294 ms | 15.16% |
    | ITL while all four requests decode | 43.104 ms | 35.921 ms | 16.66% |
    
    The DSV4.1 figures are single-round historical results.
    
    #### Profiling evidence
    
    DSV4 Flash W4A8, PCP8 + EP8, concurrency 8, 32 input / 1,024 output
    tokens; parent main `576b1b0c3` vs PR `b7645500d` (same production code
    as `8ff49a6cd`, with a CPU-test mock fix). Native torch_npu Level1
    captured 16 FULL decode steps on each of 8 ranks, while all 8 requests
    were decoding; prefill was outside the capture.
    
    | Embedding capacity-buffer copies, per rank per decode step | main | PR
    |
    |---|---:|---:|
    | Large TensorMove kernels | 5 | 0 |
    | Logical bytes copied | 1.625 GiB | 0 |
    | Summed kernel duration, mean across 8 ranks | 2.677 ms | 0 |
    
    The removed copies are three 512 MiB and two 64 MiB tensors per step.
    Embedding collective payloads are unchanged: AllGather 4 B input / 32 B
    output; ReduceScatter 64 KiB input / 8 KiB output. The gain comes from
    removing local full-buffer copies. Kernel-duration sums are not
    end-to-end latency savings; the +7.40% throughput result above was
    measured separately without profiling. The first sampled collective
    includes profiler-start/rank-arrival skew and is not used as
    steady-state communication latency.
    
    **Before:** long `aclnnInplaceCopy_TensorMoveAiCore_TensorMove` kernels
    in the embedding exchange region.
    
    <img width="2135" height="1285" alt="Before: capacity-sized TensorMove
    copies in compiled embedding exchange"
    src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/97649e5a-ef5e-487f-bc9e-c7dc56f1774f"
    />
    
    **After:** the capacity-sized copies disappear; token exchange, lookup
    and masking remain.
    
    <img width="2144" height="1043" alt="After: embedding exchange without
    capacity-sized TensorMove copies"
    src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/48f25299-d236-4d7f-a4b2-cadd82ada61f"
    />
    
    The screenshots use different zoom levels and selected windows; their
    selection durations should not be used to calculate a speedup.
    
    #### Correctness
    
    - 20 focused CPU tests passed, covering AOT functionalization/dynamic
    shapes, empty input, invalid exchange lengths and output stability
    across calls.
    - Full DSV4.1 W8A8 services on A3, DP2 × TP1 × PCP8 with EP: text and
    real-image inputs under eager, PIECEWISE and FULL_DECODE_ONLY;
    concurrency 1/3/4/8 and inputs up to 65,536 tokens. All 120 formal
    requests completed; 1,320 sampled function outputs matched CPU
    checkpoint lookup elementwise, with zero mismatches. All 2,960 profiled
    AllGather/ReduceScatter pairs matched the actual N and dtype.
    - Text graph capture/replay passed with changing inputs. Multimodal
    embedding runs outside the decoder graph; unequal rank lengths and
    cropping passed. PIECEWISE capture covered buckets through 128; larger
    prefill ran uncaptured.
    - The function-level hardware validation above used `6733d1528`. The
    subsequent in-place mask change also passed CPU correctness and AOT
    graph-equivalence checks. These function checks do not measure physical
    HCCL link bytes.
    - End-to-end GPQA Diamond on PR `8ff49a6cd`, DSV4 W4A8, the same fixed
    100 questions, concurrency 8: **77% / 71% / 77%, mean 75% (range
    71–77%)**. All 300 responses completed with zero HTTP failures/timeouts;
    prompt hashes and native per-question scoring matched. Outputs hitting
    the 8,192-token cap counted as incorrect (question 79 in all rounds;
    question 69 also in round 2). Historical results were 70% / 74% / 75%
    (mean 73%); this is reference only, since main accuracy was not rerun
    and the historical environment/version differed.
    
